  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nNorthwesterly flow aloft continues for the rest of the week and\ninto the weekend, but heights will be rising as ridging builds\nover the Rockies and Western Plains. High pressure over the CWA\ntoday will move southeast tonight, allowing south to southwest\nsurface winds to pick up. A weak shortwave tracking along the\ninternational border could bring a few isolated showers or\nstorms in our far northern tier Thursday afternoon and evening.\nHowever, an even stronger shortwave moving through on Friday \nshould push a surface trough axis into the CWA on Friday. Not\nmuch cool air advection behind the trough axis as upper ridging\ntakes over.\n\n...Fire weather concerns next few days...\n\nTemperatures will continue to be on a warming trend through the\nend of the week and into the weekend. Dew points tomorrow will\nbe similar to today, but with warmer temps there could be\nseveral spots that drop below 30 percent relative humidity.\nWhile winds will be higher than today, they are not expected to\nbe much above 15 mph and think that chances for critical fire \nweather conditions will be low, although a few locations could\nget near critical. Higher winds are expected for Friday, but dew\npoints will also be higher. The HDW index is expected to be\naround 75th percentile for Friday, but again, it looks more near\ncritical than Red Flag. \n\n...Marginal risk Friday afternoon and night...\n\nWith some forcing from the approaching upper shortwave, along\nwith good destabilization over 2000 J/kg and shear over 40 kts,\nthere is potential for some severe Friday afternoon and evening.\nThe fly in the ointment will be a fairly strong cap. 700mb temps\nclimb up into the 12 to 14 C range, and the strongest vorticity\nadvection looks to be up in Canada. However, can't rule out some\nisolated supercells, so mention of a 1 out of 5 risk continues\nto be reasonable.\n\n...Heat this weekend...\n\nThe 590dm heights push into southeastern ND this weekend. ECWMF\nEFI are up to 95th percentile for MaxT on Sunday. With the\ntrough axis passage late Friday, the dew points will not be\nquite as high as our last heat event. But still up into the 60s,\nwhich will push our heat index values in the southern forecast\narea above 100 degrees. Too soon for any heat headlines, but\nwill continue to monitor.",
